@node Overview
@chapter Overview

A news story is a set of claims that arrive over time.  Early claims
often come from whoever had the most immediate access, and later
evidence can overturn them.  Readers rarely go back and recheck what
they believed once that happens.

The system does the rechecking for you.  You record each piece of
evidence with its source and the moment it became public, and you write
rules that say how evidence supports conclusions.  The engine,
@command{colloquy}, then works out which conclusions stand, at any
moment you choose.

It never gives a probability.  Every verdict is one of five statuses:

@table @asis
@item ACCEPTED
Some argument for the claim survives every attack.
@item REFUTED
Some argument for a contrary claim is accepted.
@item DEFEATED
Arguments for the claim exist, but none survives.  This means
@emph{not established}, which is different from shown false.
@item CONTESTED
Arguments for and against defeat each other and neither side wins.
@item -
Nothing supports the claim yet.
@end table

Because every verdict comes with the arguments behind it, you can
always ask @emph{why}, and you can always ask @emph{what would change
it}.  Those two questions are the point of the system.

@node Installing
@chapter Installing

These steps are for Debian 12 or 13, or Ubuntu 24.04.

@enumerate
@item
Put the three @file{.deb} files in one folder, open a terminal there,
and install them:

@example
sudo apt install ./colloquy_0.3.0_all.deb \
  ./news-analysis-cases_0.3.0_all.deb \
  ./news-analysis-system_0.3.0_all.deb
@end example

It worked if the output includes @samp{Setting up news-analysis-system}.
apt also installs SWI-Prolog if you do not have it.

@item
Check the engine and the cases:

@example
colloquy version
colloquy cases
@end example

Expect @samp{colloquy 0.3.0}, then three case names.
@end enumerate

Once installed, this tutorial is in the Info directory: run
@command{info news-analysis}, or in Emacs press @kbd{C-h i} and choose
@samp{News Analysis}.  The proposal paper is installed as
@file{/usr/share/doc/news-analysis-system/news-analysis-system.pdf}.

@node First Steps
@chapter First Steps

Run the whole demo first, to see what output looks like:

@example
news-analysis-demo | less
@end example

Now ask a question yourself.  The fake Pentagon image case covers one
morning in May 2023.  Ask for the state of play at 10:06, when the image
first spread:

@example
colloquy status pentagon-fake-image --at 2023-05-22T10:06
@end example

You will see:

@example
  ACCEPTED   explosion_at_pentagon
             Was there an explosion at the Pentagon?
  ACCEPTED   image_is_authentic
             Is the viral image a real photograph of the scene?
  -          markets_reacted_to_claim
             Did markets react to the claim?
@end example

At 10:06, with only the image public, the engine accepts the explosion.
That is not a bug.  Nothing yet contradicts the image, so on the
evidence available it stands.  Now ask again without @option{--at},
which means ``as of the latest evidence'':

@example
colloquy status pentagon-fake-image
@end example

Both claims are now REFUTED, and the market reaction is ACCEPTED: the
markets did move, even though the event did not happen.

Two more questions are worth trying straight away:

@example
colloquy whatif pentagon-fake-image explosion_at_pentagon \
  --at 2023-05-22T10:06
colloquy explain pentagon-fake-image explosion_at_pentagon
@end example

The first lists what a careful reader should have asked at 10:06.  The
second shows, step by step, why the explosion claim is refuted now.

@node Core Ideas
@chapter Core Ideas

The engine implements a framework from the research literature called
ASPIC+.  You do not need to know the literature.  This chapter explains
the six ideas it rests on, using small examples.

@section Evidence, with dates
A @dfn{premise} is a statement of record: something a source said, or
something a measurement showed, at a known time.  For example:

@example
viral_photo_of_collapsed_bridge     public 2026-03-01T08:00
city_says_bridge_open               public 2026-03-01T08:30
@end example

A premise records that the source said it, not that it is true.
@samp{city_says_bridge_open} means the city said the bridge is open.
Whether the bridge @emph{is} open is a conclusion the engine reaches.

Each premise carries the date, or date and time, it became public.
When you ask about a moment with @option{--at}, premises from later are
simply not there.  That is how the engine replays the past.

@section Rules
A @dfn{rule} says that some premises or conclusions support another
conclusion.  There are two kinds:

@table @asis
@item Strict rules
If the antecedents hold, the conclusion must hold.  Use these rarely,
for definitions.
@item Defeasible rules
If the antecedents hold, the conclusion presumably holds, unless
something defeats it.  Almost every rule about news is defeasible.
@end table

For example, ``the authority responsible for the bridge reports it
open, so presumably it is open'' is defeasible: the authority could be
wrong.

@section Arguments
An @dfn{argument} is a chain of rules ending at premises.  The engine
builds every argument the case allows.  For instance:

@example
bridge_collapsed              by rule r_photo_shows
    viral_photo_of_collapsed_bridge   (premise)
    photo_is_current          by rule r_photo_current
        viral_photo_of_collapsed_bridge   (premise)
@end example

Each sub-chain is itself an argument, a @dfn{sub-argument}.  This
matters because attacking any sub-argument attacks the whole.

@section Three ways to attack an argument
@table @asis
@item Rebut
Argue for the opposite of a defeasible conclusion.  The city's
statement, read as ``the bridge did not collapse'', rebuts the photo
argument.
@item Undercut
Argue that a rule does not apply here, without claiming the opposite.
In the OPERA case, the discovery of a loose cable does not show that
neutrinos are slower than light; it shows that the measurement cannot
be used.  In a case file, an undercut is a rule whose conclusion is
@samp{undercut(@var{rule-id})}.
@item Undermine
Argue that a premise is false: the source did not say what we recorded,
or the record is wrong.
@end table

Opposites are written with @samp{neg(@dots{})}.  Two claims that
cannot both hold, without one being the negation of the other, are
declared with @samp{contrary(A, B)}.

@section Which attack wins
An attack only succeeds, and becomes a @dfn{defeat}, if the attacker is
not weaker than what it attacks.  Undercuts always succeed.  Rebuts and
undermines are compared by @dfn{priority}.

Every defeasible rule and premise has a priority, an integer where
higher means a more reliable kind of evidence.  The demo cases use
roughly this scale:

@example
1  an inference from behaviour with other explanations,
   an anonymous post, unmeasured impressions
2  testimony by an interested party
3  official records, forensic reports
4  independent measurement or replication
5  recordings, an experimenter's report of their own error
6  court verdicts
@end example

These are an ordinal ranking of kinds of evidence, not certainties.

To compare two arguments, the engine looks at the @emph{last}
defeasible rule each used to reach its conclusion (the
@dfn{last-link} principle).  At 08:30 in the bridge case, the photo
argument ends in @samp{r_photo_shows} (priority 1) and the city
argument ends in @samp{r_city} (priority 3).  So the city defeats the
photo argument, and the photo argument does not defeat the city.

When both sides have equal priority, each defeats the other.  That is
what happens in the OPERA case in September 2011: a priority-3
measurement against a priority-3 theory.

@section Deciding what stands
Defeats form a network: A defeats B, B defeats C, and so on.  The
engine labels each argument IN, OUT or UNDECIDED using
@dfn{grounded semantics}, the most cautious standard in the field:

@enumerate
@item
An argument is IN if every argument that defeats it is already OUT
(including when nothing defeats it).
@item
An argument defeated by an IN argument is OUT.
@item
Repeat until nothing changes.  Anything left is UNDECIDED.
@end enumerate

In the bridge case at 08:30, the city argument has no attackers that
succeed, so it is IN.  The photo argument is defeated by an IN argument,
so it is OUT.

In OPERA in September 2011, the measurement and the theory defeat each
other and nothing else touches them.  Neither ever gets labelled, so
both stay UNDECIDED, and the claim is CONTESTED.  In February 2012 the
fault report undercuts the measurement.  The undercut has no attackers,
so it is IN; the measurement becomes OUT; the theory now has no
undefeated attacker, so it becomes IN; and the claim is REFUTED.

@section The five statuses again
A claim's status comes from the labels of the arguments for it and
against it:

@itemize @bullet
@item
Any argument for it is IN: ACCEPTED.
@item
Otherwise, any argument for a contrary is IN: REFUTED.
@item
Otherwise, any argument for it is UNDECIDED: CONTESTED.
@item
Otherwise, arguments for it exist but are all OUT: DEFEATED.
@item
Otherwise: no argument at all, shown as @samp{-}.
@end itemize

The difference between REFUTED and DEFEATED matters.  In the Yandex
case, the claim that political bot activity fell is DEFEATED: every
argument for it was undercut, because none of the evidence offered
actually measures it.  Nothing shows that bot activity did @emph{not}
fall.  The honest verdict is ``not established''.

@node A Case From Scratch
@chapter A Case From Scratch

This chapter builds a small fictional case, the Riverside Bridge
rumour.  The finished file is installed as
@file{/usr/share/doc/news-analysis-system/examples/bridge-rumour.pl}
(in the source tree, @file{doc/examples/bridge-rumour.pl}); you will
write it yourself here.

@section Step 1: sources and the helper
Create @file{~/bridge.pl} and start with the title and the sources:

@example
case_title('Tutorial: the Riverside Bridge rumour (fictional)').

src(post,  'A social media post (fictional)',              'none').
src(city,  'City traffic department account (fictional)', 'none').
src(check, 'Reverse image search result (fictional)',      'none').
src(tv,    'Local TV live broadcast (fictional)',          'none').

premise(Id, L, [date(D), source(S), url(U), prio(P), note(N)|X]) :-
    p(Id, L, D, K, P, N, X),
    src(K, S, U).
@end example

The @samp{premise} clause is a helper: it lets you write each piece of
evidence as one short @samp{p/7} fact and attaches the source for you.
Copy it unchanged into every case.

@section Step 2: the questions
Say which claims the case is about:

@example
topic(bridge_collapsed, 'Did the Riverside Bridge collapse?').
topic(photo_is_current, 'Is the viral photo from today?').
@end example

@section Step 3: the first piece of evidence and its rules
@example
p(photo, viral_photo_of_collapsed_bridge, '2026-03-01T08:00', post, 1,
  'A post shows a collapsed bridge captioned "Riverside Bridge is gone"',
  []).

defeasible(r_photo_current, [viral_photo_of_collapsed_bridge],
           photo_is_current,
           [prio(1), label('a photo posted as news is presumed to be from today')]).
critical_question(r_photo_current, 'Has the photo appeared anywhere before?').

defeasible(r_photo_shows, [viral_photo_of_collapsed_bridge, photo_is_current],
           bridge_collapsed,
           [prio(1), label('a current photo shows what happened')]).
@end example

The arguments of @samp{p/7} are: an identifier, the literal, the time,
the source key, the priority, a one-line note for the timeline, and a
list of extras (use @samp{[]}).

Run it:

@example
colloquy status ~/bridge.pl
@end example

Both topics are ACCEPTED.  With one photo and nothing else, the engine
has no reason to doubt it.  Ask what would change that:

@example
colloquy whatif ~/bridge.pl bridge_collapsed
@end example

It lists the photo premise, both rules, and the critical question you
attached.  This list is the reader's homework at 08:00.

@section Step 4: the authority speaks
Add the city's statement and a rule for it:

@example
p(city, city_says_bridge_open, '2026-03-01T08:30', city, 3,
  'City traffic department: bridge open, a minor accident was cleared',
  []).

defeasible(r_city, [city_says_bridge_open], neg(bridge_collapsed),
           [prio(3), label('the authority responsible for the bridge reports it open')]).
@end example

Run @command{colloquy timeline ~/bridge.pl}.  At 08:30,
@samp{bridge_collapsed} goes from ACCEPTED to REFUTED: a priority-3
rebut beats a priority-1 argument.  But @samp{photo_is_current} is
still ACCEPTED, because nothing has attacked that part yet.

@section Step 5: the photo is old
@example
p(search, photo_found_online_in_2019, '2026-03-01T08:45', check, 3,
  'The same photo appears online in 2019, from a different country',
  []).

defeasible(r_old_photo, [photo_found_online_in_2019], neg(photo_is_current),
           [prio(3), label('a photo published in 2019 is not from today')]).
@end example

Now at 08:45 @samp{photo_is_current} is REFUTED too.  The critical
question from step 3 has been answered.

@section Step 6: evidence that changes nothing
@example
p(live, live_tv_shows_traffic_on_bridge, '2026-03-01T09:00', tv, 4,
  'Live TV shows traffic crossing the bridge',
  []).

defeasible(r_live, [live_tv_shows_traffic_on_bridge], neg(bridge_collapsed),
           [prio(4), label('a live broadcast shows the bridge standing')]).
@end example

The timeline at 09:00 says @samp{(no change in any conclusion)}.  The
claim was already refuted; the live broadcast is a second, independent
reason.  @command{colloquy explain ~/bridge.pl bridge_collapsed} now
shows two accepted arguments against the rumour.

@section What you built
@example
2026-03-01T08:00   bridge_collapsed: - -> ACCEPTED
                   photo_is_current: - -> ACCEPTED
2026-03-01T08:30   bridge_collapsed: ACCEPTED -> REFUTED
2026-03-01T08:45   photo_is_current: ACCEPTED -> REFUTED
2026-03-01T09:00   (no change in any conclusion)
@end example

That is the whole method.  Real cases differ only in having more
evidence, more careful rules, and real sources with URLs.

@node Commands
@chapter Commands

Every command takes a @var{case}: either a file path, or the name of an
installed case such as @samp{opera-neutrinos}.  Most take
@option{--at @var{date}}, where @var{date} is @samp{YYYY-MM-DD} or
@samp{YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM}.  Without it, the engine uses the latest
evidence in the case.

@table @code
@item colloquy status @var{case} [--at @var{date}]
The status of every topic in the case.

@item colloquy explain @var{case} @var{literal} [--at @var{date}]
Every argument for @var{literal}, labelled IN, OUT or UNDECIDED, with
its premises and sources, and the arguments that defeat it.

@item colloquy whatif @var{case} @var{literal} [--at @var{date}]
For an accepted claim: the premises, rules and critical questions whose
defeat would change it.  For a refuted claim: the points in the winning
argument that would have to fall.  For a claim with no argument: which
rules could conclude it.

@item colloquy timeline @var{case}
Steps through each moment at which evidence arrived, listing the new
evidence and every status that changed.

@item colloquy robustness @var{case} [--at @var{date}] [--max @var{n}]
For each topic, the conspiracy number and, for a topic not accepted,
how many sources would have to be set aside for it to become accepted;
then every verdict that changes if one priority moves by one.
@xref{Robustness}.

@item colloquy aif @var{case} [--at @var{date}]
Writes the argument graph as AIF JSON, the Argument Interchange Format
used by other argumentation tools.

@item colloquy cases
Lists the installed cases.  Set @env{COLLOQUY_CASE_PATH} to a
colon-separated list of directories to add your own.

@item news-analysis-demo
Replays all three demo cases and shows one @samp{whatif} and one
@samp{explain}.
@end table

Literals are written as Prolog terms.  Put quotes around any that
contain parentheses, for example @samp{'neg(bridge_collapsed)'}.

@node The Demo Cases
@chapter The Demo Cases

The three installed cases were chosen to end three different ways.

@section pentagon-fake-image: refuted within the day
On 22 May 2023 an apparently AI-generated image of smoke ``at the
Pentagon'' spread on Twitter at 10:06, and US stocks fell sharply for a
few minutes.  The Arlington County fire department and the Pentagon
Force Protection Agency said there was no incident, and an analyst at
Bellingcat pointed out artifacts in the image and the absence of any
other witnesses.

What to look at: @command{colloquy whatif} at 10:06 lists the three
questions the afternoon's evidence answered.  The market reaction stays
ACCEPTED throughout: it shows belief, not truth.

@section opera-neutrinos: contested, then self-corrected
In September 2011 the OPERA experiment reported neutrinos arriving
about 60 ns earlier than light would.  The engine holds this as
CONTESTED against special relativity, rather than accepting or
dismissing it.  A November re-test on the same apparatus changes
nothing.  In February 2012 the team found a loose fibre-optic link and a
fast-running clock, and the claim becomes REFUTED.

What to look at: the timeline after February 2012.  An independent
replication, the resignations of the experiment's leaders, and the
final corrected results all change nothing.  The first is redundant;
the second is not evidence; the third confirms what was already known.

@section yandex-datacentres-bots: not established (live)
In October 2026 Ukrainian drones struck Yandex data centres, and
claims spread that pro-Kremlin bot activity on Western social media fell
afterwards.  The engine accepts that the strikes happened (both sides
describe them) and that automated traffic from Yandex's network fell.
It finds the claim that political bot activity fell DEFEATED: the
testimony offered cites no data, the user reports are unmeasured, and
the traffic figure also counts legitimate crawlers.  A popular version
naming pro-Trump bots specifically has no argument at all, because no
source checked makes that claim.

What to look at: @command{colloquy explain yandex-datacentres-bots
russian_political_bot_activity_fell}.  Three arguments, each undercut
for a different reason.

This case is live.  As new evidence appears, add it and rerun the
timeline.

@node Experiments
@chapter Experiments

@section Experiment 1: what evidence would establish the bot claim?
@enumerate
@item
Copy the case:

@example
cp /usr/share/news-analysis-system/cases/yandex-datacentres-bots.pl \
   ~/yandex-test.pl
@end example

@item
Append a hypothetical independent study:

@example
cat >> ~/yandex-test.pl <<'EOF'

src(hyp, 'HYPOTHETICAL evidence for experimenting', 'none').
p(study, study_measures_political_bot_drop, '2026-10-20', hyp, 4,
  'HYPOTHETICAL: an independent study measures a drop in bot posts', []).
defeasible(r_study, [study_measures_political_bot_drop],
           russian_political_bot_activity_fell,
           [prio(4), scheme(measurement), label('an independent measurement')]).
EOF
@end example

@item
Replay it with @command{colloquy timeline ~/yandex-test.pl}.  It worked
if 2026-10-20 shows @samp{russian_political_bot_activity_fell: DEFEATED
-> ACCEPTED}.  The causal claim becomes ACCEPTED too, because its other
premise (the strikes) was already established.
@end enumerate

The lesson: more testimony would not change the verdict.  Measurement
would.

@section Experiment 2: how much do priorities matter?
@enumerate
@item
Copy the OPERA case and lower the theory's priority from 3 to 2:

@example
cp /usr/share/news-analysis-system/cases/opera-neutrinos.pl ~/opera-test.pl
sed -i 's/\[prio(3), scheme(established_theory)/[prio(2), scheme(established_theory)/' \
    ~/opera-test.pl
@end example

@item
Run @command{colloquy timeline ~/opera-test.pl}.  It worked if
2011-09-23 now shows ACCEPTED instead of CONTESTED.
@end enumerate

One number changed the verdict.  This is why every priority in a case
should be visible and open to challenge, and why the cases use a
published scale rather than numbers chosen per claim.

@section Experiment 3: an unfalsifiable claim
Add a premise and a rule to any copied case that conclude a new topic,
without any evidence that could ever count against it.  Then run
@command{colloquy whatif} on it.  Notice that the only attack points are
the premise itself and the rule.  If no conceivable observation could
undermine the premise or undercut the rule, the claim can never be
tested, and the engine's verdict on it says nothing about the world.
That is the reason for the critical question ``what observation would
count against this?''.

@node Robustness
@chapter Robustness

A verdict is only as strong as what it rests on.  The
@command{robustness} command asks two questions of every topic.

@section Conspiracy numbers
The @dfn{conspiracy number} of a claim is the smallest number of
sources that would have to be wrong, mistaken or colluding for its
status to change.  The engine finds it by setting sources aside, one,
then two, then three, and recomputing.  The name comes from
McAllester's conspiracy numbers for game-tree search, which count how
many leaves must change to change the value at the root.

@example
colloquy robustness pentagon-fake-image
@end example

@example
explosion_at_pentagon  REFUTED
  conspiracy number 1: set aside [official] and it becomes DEFEATED
  to become ACCEPTED: set aside 3: [analysis,impostor,official]
@end example

Read it like this.  If the officials' statement were wrong, the claim
of an explosion would no longer be refuted, but it would still not be
established (DEFEATED), because the image analysis would still sink the
photograph.  For the explosion to be @emph{accepted}, three separate
sources would all have to be wrong: the officials, the image analyst,
and the record of who spread the image.

The measure treats both directions alike.  A claim that one interested
party is lying has a conspiracy number of one, and suspecting it is
reasonable.  A claim that needs many independent sources to be
coordinated has a high number, and the engine says so without calling
it absurd.

Two results in the demo cases are worth noticing:

@itemize @bullet
@item
In the Yandex case, @samp{russian_political_bot_activity_fell} becomes
ACCEPTED if one source is set aside: the caveat that the traffic figure
also counts crawlers.  The claim is one fact away from established, and
that fact is the thing to check.
@item
@samp{pro_trump_bots_specifically_reduced} cannot become accepted by
setting any sources aside.  Nothing supports it; only new evidence
could.
@end itemize

By default each premise is its own source.  When several premises come
from one origin (one outlet repeating another, or one person quoted in
several places), add @samp{origin(@var{O})} to their metadata and they
count once.

@section Priority sensitivity
The second part of the report moves each priority up and down by one
and lists every verdict that changes.  On the latest evidence the demo
cases are stable: no single move changes anything.  Earlier moments can
be fragile:

@example
colloquy robustness opera-neutrinos --at 2011-09-23
@end example

@example
Priority sensitivity at 2011-09-23 (each priority moved by one):
  r_measure 3 -> 2: neutrinos_faster_than_light CONTESTED -> REFUTED
  r_measure 3 -> 4: neutrinos_faster_than_light CONTESTED -> ACCEPTED
  r_theory 3 -> 2: neutrinos_faster_than_light CONTESTED -> ACCEPTED
  r_theory 3 -> 4: neutrinos_faster_than_light CONTESTED -> REFUTED
@end example

In September 2011 the verdict rested entirely on judging the
measurement and the theory equally reliable.  That is honest, and the
report makes the dependence visible: anyone who disputes the verdict
can see exactly which judgement to argue about.

@node Case File Reference
@chapter Case File Reference

A case file is ordinary Prolog, loaded into its own module.  These are
the predicates the engine reads:

@table @code
@item case_title(@var{Text})
The title printed by @command{status} and @command{timeline}.

@item premise(@var{Id}, @var{Literal}, @var{Meta})
An attackable statement of record.  @var{Meta} is a list that may
contain @samp{date(D)}, @samp{source(S)}, @samp{url(U)},
@samp{prio(P)}, @samp{note(N)}, @samp{precision(P)} and
@samp{origin(O)}.  A premise with no date is available at every moment;
use that for background knowledge.  Premises with the same
@samp{origin} count as one source for conspiracy numbers.

@item axiom(@var{Id}, @var{Literal}, @var{Meta})
A statement that cannot be attacked.  Use very sparingly.

@item defeasible(@var{Id}, @var{Antecedents}, @var{Conclusion}, @var{Meta})
A defeasible rule.  @var{Meta} may contain @samp{prio(P)},
@samp{scheme(S)} and @samp{label(Text)}.

@item strict(@var{Id}, @var{Antecedents}, @var{Conclusion})
A strict rule.

@item critical_question(@var{RuleId}, @var{Text})
A question a reader should ask before trusting the rule.  Shown by
@command{whatif}.

@item contrary(@var{A}, @var{B})
@var{A} and @var{B} cannot both hold.  Symmetric.

@item topic(@var{Literal}, @var{Description})
A question the case tracks.  If a case declares no topics, the engine
uses every defeasible conclusion.
@end table

Dates are atoms: @samp{'2026-10-12'} or @samp{'2026-10-12T14:30'}.
When a source gives no time for a statement, use the time the source
was published and add @samp{precision(upper_bound)}: the statement was
public by then, possibly earlier.  Use @samp{precision(approximate)} or
@samp{precision(month)} where those apply.

@node How It Works
@chapter How It Works

The engine is one file, @file{/usr/share/colloquy/colloquy.pl}, about
670 lines of SWI-Prolog.  For a query at moment @var{t}:

@enumerate
@item
Load the case into its own module.  (@code{load_case/1})

@item
Collect the premises public at or before @var{t}; each is a one-step
argument.  (@code{arguments_at/2})

@item
Repeatedly apply every rule whose antecedents are concluded by existing
arguments, building larger arguments, until nothing new appears.
Arguments that would use their own conclusion are skipped.
(@code{closure/2})

@item
For every pair of arguments, check every sub-argument for an undermine,
rebut or undercut, and keep the attacks that succeed under the last-link
priority comparison.  (@code{defeat/4}, @code{weaker/2})

@item
Label arguments IN, OUT or UNDECIDED by grounded semantics.
(@code{grounded/6})

@item
Turn argument labels into the five statuses.  (@code{literal_status/3})
@end enumerate

A what-if scenario (premises set aside, priorities changed) is held in
a global variable and is part of every cached key; the
@command{robustness} command uses it to recompute without editing the
case.  Results for each case, moment and scenario are memoised with
tabling.  The engine
never uses assert or retract.

@node Limitations
@chapter Limitations

@itemize @bullet
@item
Only grounded semantics.  Preferred semantics, which would list every
coherent interpretation the evidence allows, is planned.
@item
Priorities are set by hand.  The @command{robustness} command shows
which verdicts depend on them, but cannot tell you whether they are
right.
@item
Conspiracy numbers only consider setting existing sources aside, and
count sources as independent unless told otherwise with
@samp{origin}.  The search tries every combination, so keep
@option{--max} small for large cases.
@item
No automatic extraction.  Cases are written by hand; an LLM-based
extractor that proposes premises and rules for review is planned.
@item
No archive.  URLs point at live pages; linking each premise to a dated,
hashed capture is planned.
@item
Argument construction is exhaustive, which is fine for cases of a few
dozen premises and rules but will need indexing for large ones.
@item
The case format is ad hoc Prolog.  An RDF/OWL format is under
consideration.  See @file{ROADMAP.org} in the source tree for plans.
@end itemize

@bye
